::ALG2_U01_L02_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L02_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.2; MLA.A2.AE.4<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify (4x<sup>2</sup> + 3x - 8) + (2x<sup>2</sup> - 5x + 1). {
~6x<sup>2</sup> + 8x - 7 #This adds 3x and 5x instead of combining 3x and -5x.
=6x<sup>2</sup> - 2x - 7 #Correct. Combine like terms: 4x squared plus 2x squared, 3x minus 5x, and -8 plus 1.
~8x<sup>2</sup> - 2x - 7 #This multiplies the x squared coefficients instead of adding them.
~6x<sup>2</sup> - 2x + 9 #This adds the constants incorrectly.
}

::ALG2_U01_L02_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L02_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.2; MLA.A2.AE.4<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify (7x<sup>3</sup> - 2x + 4) + (-3x<sup>3</sup> + 5x - 9). {
~10x<sup>3</sup> + 3x - 5 #This adds 7x cubed and 3x cubed instead of using -3x cubed.
=4x<sup>3</sup> + 3x - 5 #Correct. 7x cubed minus 3x cubed is 4x cubed, -2x plus 5x is 3x, and 4 minus 9 is -5.
~4x<sup>6</sup> + 3x - 5 #This incorrectly adds exponents when adding like terms.
~4x<sup>3</sup> - 7x - 5 #This combines the x terms incorrectly.
}

::ALG2_U01_L02_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L02_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.2; MLA.A2.AE.4<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify (5x<sup>2</sup> - 4x + 6) - (2x<sup>2</sup> + x - 3). {
~3x<sup>2</sup> - 3x + 3 #This does not distribute the subtraction sign to every term in the second polynomial.
=3x<sup>2</sup> - 5x + 9 #Correct. Subtract every term in the second polynomial, then combine like terms.
~7x<sup>2</sup> - 5x + 9 #This adds the x squared terms instead of subtracting.
~3x<sup>2</sup> + 5x + 9 #The x-term sign is incorrect.
}

::ALG2_U01_L02_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L02_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.2; MLA.A2.AE.4<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify (8x<sup>2</sup> + 3x - 1) - (x<sup>2</sup> - 6x + 5). {
=7x<sup>2</sup> + 9x - 6 #Correct. Distribute the subtraction sign to get -x squared + 6x - 5, then combine.
~7x<sup>2</sup> - 3x - 6 #This does not change the sign of -6x when subtracting.
~9x<sup>2</sup> + 9x - 6 #This adds x squared instead of subtracting it.
~7x<sup>2</sup> + 9x + 4 #This does not subtract the constant 5 correctly.
}

::ALG2_U01_L02_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L02_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.2; MLA.A2.AE.4<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 4x(3x<sup>2</sup> - 2x + 7). {
~12x<sup>2</sup> - 8x + 28 #This does not add the exponent from the outside x.
=12x<sup>3</sup> - 8x<sup>2</sup> + 28x #Correct. Distribute 4x to each term.
~12x<sup>3</sup> - 2x<sup>2</sup> + 7x #This does not multiply every coefficient by 4.
~12x<sup>3</sup> + 8x<sup>2</sup> + 28x #The middle term should be negative.
}

::ALG2_U01_L02_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L02_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.2; MLA.A2.AE.4<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify -2x(5x<sup>2</sup> - 3x + 4). {
~-10x<sup>2</sup> + 6x - 8 #This does not add the exponent from the outside x.
=-10x<sup>3</sup> + 6x<sup>2</sup> - 8x #Correct. Multiply each term by -2x.
~-10x<sup>3</sup> - 6x<sup>2</sup> - 8x #The middle term should be positive because a negative times a negative is positive.
~10x<sup>3</sup> - 6x<sup>2</sup> + 8x #This ignores the negative sign on -2x.
}

::ALG2_U01_L02_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L02_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.2; MLA.A2.AE.4<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify (x + 7)(x + 2). {
~x<sup>2</sup> + 14 #This multiplies only the first and last terms.
=x<sup>2</sup> + 9x + 14 #Correct. The middle terms are 2x and 7x, which combine to 9x.
~x<sup>2</sup> + 5x + 14 #The middle terms were subtracted instead of added.
~x<sup>2</sup> + 14x + 9 #The constant and middle coefficient are switched.
}

::ALG2_U01_L02_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L02_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.2; MLA.A2.AE.4<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify (x - 5)(x + 3). {
~x<sup>2</sup> - 15 #This misses the middle products.
~x<sup>2</sup> + 8x - 15 #This treats -5x as +5x.
=x<sup>2</sup> - 2x - 15 #Correct. x squared + 3x - 5x - 15 combines to x squared - 2x - 15.
~x<sup>2</sup> + 2x - 15 #The middle term sign is incorrect.
}

::ALG2_U01_L02_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L02_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.2; MLA.A2.AE.4<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify (2x + 1)(x + 6). {
=2x<sup>2</sup> + 13x + 6 #Correct. 2x times x is 2x squared, 2x times 6 is 12x, and 1 times x is x.
~2x<sup>2</sup> + 12x + 6 #This misses the 1 times x product.
~2x<sup>2</sup> + 7x + 6 #This adds 6x and x instead of 12x and x.
~2x<sup>2</sup> + 13x + 7 #The constant should be 1 times 6, which is 6.
}

::ALG2_U01_L02_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L02_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.2; MLA.A2.AE.4<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify (3x - 4)(x - 2). {
~3x<sup>2</sup> - 6x + 8 #This misses the -4x product.
=3x<sup>2</sup> - 10x + 8 #Correct. 3x squared - 6x - 4x + 8 combines to 3x squared - 10x + 8.
~3x<sup>2</sup> + 10x + 8 #The middle terms should be negative.
~3x<sup>2</sup> - 2x + 8 #The middle terms were combined incorrectly.
}

::ALG2_U01_L02_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L02_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.2; MLA.A2.AE.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which pair of terms are like terms? {
~3x<sup>2</sup> and 3x #The exponents on x are different.
=-5x<sup>3</sup> and 8x<sup>3</sup> #Correct. Both terms have x raised to the third power.
~7x and 7y #The variables are different.
~4 and 4x #One term has a variable and the other does not.
}

::ALG2_U01_L02_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L02_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.2; MLA.A2.AE.4<br><br>Question:<br>What is the degree of 6x<sup>4</sup> - 2x<sup>2</sup> + 9? {
~6 #This is the leading coefficient, not the degree.
=4 #Correct. The highest exponent is 4.
~2 #This is an exponent in the expression, but it is not the highest exponent.
~9 #This is the constant term, not the degree.
}

::ALG2_U01_L02_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L02_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.2; MLA.A2.AE.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression is written in standard form? {
~5 + 2x<sup>2</sup> - x #Standard form writes terms in descending order by exponent.
=2x<sup>3</sup> - 4x<sup>2</sup> + x - 8 #Correct. The exponents decrease from 3 to 2 to 1 to 0.
~x - 3x<sup>2</sup> + 7 #The x squared term should come before the x term.
~4 - x + x<sup>4</sup> #The x to the fourth term should come first.
}

::ALG2_U01_L02_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L02_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.2; MLA.A2.AE.4<br><br>Question:<br>A rectangle has length x + 9 and width x + 4. What polynomial represents the area? {
~2x + 13 #This adds length and width instead of multiplying.
=x<sup>2</sup> + 13x + 36 #Correct. Area is (x + 9)(x + 4).
~x<sup>2</sup> + 36 #This misses the middle products.
~x<sup>2</sup> - 13x + 36 #Both constants are positive, so the middle term should be positive.
}

::ALG2_U01_L02_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L02_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.2; MLA.A2.AE.4<br><br>Question:<br>A rectangle has length 2x + 5 and width x + 3. What polynomial represents the area? {
~3x + 8 #This adds the side lengths instead of multiplying them.
~2x<sup>2</sup> + 15 #This multiplies only the first and last terms.
=2x<sup>2</sup> + 11x + 15 #Correct. (2x + 5)(x + 3) = 2x squared + 6x + 5x + 15.
~2x<sup>2</sup> + 6x + 15 #This misses the 5x product.
}

::ALG2_U01_L02_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L02_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.2; MLA.A2.AE.4<br><br>Question:<br>Find the perimeter of a rectangle with length 3x + 2 and width x + 5. {
~4x + 7 #This adds length and width once, not the full perimeter.
=8x + 14 #Correct. Perimeter is 2(3x + 2) + 2(x + 5) = 8x + 14.
~6x + 4 #This doubles only the length.
~6x<sup>2</sup> + 19x + 10 #This is an area expression, not perimeter.
}

::ALG2_U01_L02_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L02_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.2; MLA.A2.AE.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression is equivalent to (x + 4)(x + 4)? {
~x<sup>2</sup> + 16 #This misses the two middle products.
~x<sup>2</sup> + 8 #This adds the constants but does not multiply the binomials.
=x<sup>2</sup> + 8x + 16 #Correct. (x + 4)(x + 4) = x squared + 4x + 4x + 16.
~x<sup>2</sup> - 8x + 16 #The middle term should be positive.
}

::ALG2_U01_L02_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L02_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.2; MLA.A2.AE.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression is equivalent to (x - 3)<sup>2</sup>? {
~x<sup>2</sup> - 9 #This misses the middle term.
=x<sup>2</sup> - 6x + 9 #Correct. (x - 3)(x - 3) gives x squared - 6x + 9.
~x<sup>2</sup> + 6x + 9 #This has the middle term sign wrong.
~x<sup>2</sup> - 3x + 9 #This includes only one middle product.
}

::ALG2_U01_L02_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L02_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.2; MLA.A2.AE.4<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify (x + 5)(x - 5). {
~x<sup>2</sup> + 25 #The last product is negative because 5 times -5 is -25.
=x<sup>2</sup> - 25 #Correct. The middle terms -5x and 5x cancel.
~x<sup>2</sup> - 10x - 25 #The middle terms should cancel.
~x<sup>2</sup> + 10x - 25 #The middle terms should cancel.
}

::ALG2_U01_L02_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L02_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.2; MLA.A2.AE.4<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify (2x + 3)(2x - 3). {
=4x<sup>2</sup> - 9 #Correct. The middle terms -6x and 6x cancel.
~4x<sup>2</sup> + 9 #The last product is negative because 3 times -3 is -9.
~4x<sup>2</sup> - 12x - 9 #The middle terms should cancel.
~2x<sup>2</sup> - 9 #2x times 2x is 4x squared, not 2x squared.
}

::ALG2_U01_L02_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L02_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.2; MLA.A2.AE.4<br><br>Question:<br>A student simplifies 4x<sup>2</sup> + 3x as 7x<sup>2</sup>. What is the best correction? {
~The student should write 7x<sup>3</sup> because exponents add. #Exponents do not add when adding terms.
=The terms are unlike terms, so they cannot be combined. #Correct. x squared and x are different powers.
~The student should write 12x<sup>3</sup> because coefficients multiply. #This is not multiplication of terms.
~The expression should equal x because 4 plus 3 is 7. #This does not preserve the original terms.
}

::ALG2_U01_L02_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L02_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.2; MLA.A2.AE.4<br><br>Question:<br>A student simplifies (x + 6)(x + 2) as x<sup>2</sup> + 12. What did the student most likely forget? {
~To multiply the constants #The constants were multiplied to get 12.
~To write the leading term #The leading term x squared is present.
=To include the middle products 2x and 6x #Correct. The full product includes x squared + 2x + 6x + 12.
~To subtract the second polynomial #This is multiplication, not subtraction.
}

::ALG2_U01_L02_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L02_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.2; MLA.A2.AE.4<br><br>Question:<br>A student simplifies (6x<sup>2</sup> + 4x - 1) - (2x<sup>2</sup> - x + 5) as 4x<sup>2</sup> + 3x + 4. What error occurred? {
~The student added the polynomials instead of subtracting. #The x squared term shows subtraction, but the signs in the second polynomial were not all changed correctly.
=The student did not distribute the subtraction sign to every term in the second polynomial. #Correct. The correct result is 4x squared + 5x - 6.
~The student multiplied the polynomials. #The result does not come from multiplying the expressions.
~The student changed the first polynomial instead of the second. #The first polynomial stayed unchanged.
}

::ALG2_U01_L02_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L02_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.2; MLA.A2.AE.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression represents the sum of 2x<sup>2</sup> - 3x + 4 and x<sup>2</sup> + 8x - 10? {
~3x<sup>2</sup> - 11x - 6 #The x terms were subtracted instead of added.
=3x<sup>2</sup> + 5x - 6 #Correct. Add like terms from both polynomials.
~2x<sup>4</sup> + 5x - 6 #This incorrectly multiplies or combines exponents.
~3x<sup>2</sup> + 5x + 14 #The constants should combine to -6.
}

::ALG2_U01_L02_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L02_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.2; MLA.A2.AE.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression represents the difference of 9x<sup>2</sup> + 2x - 7 and 4x<sup>2</sup> - 6x + 1? {
=5x<sup>2</sup> + 8x - 8 #Correct. Subtract the second polynomial: 9x squared + 2x - 7 - 4x squared + 6x - 1.
~5x<sup>2</sup> - 4x - 6 #This does not distribute the subtraction sign to each term.
~13x<sup>2</sup> - 4x - 6 #This adds the x squared terms instead of subtracting.
~5x<sup>2</sup> + 8x + 8 #The constant term should be -8.
}
